## 6. Validation Pipeline

### 6.1 Purpose

Every problem in the bank must be: (a) well-formed (system doesn't diverge, data has structure), (b) non-trivial (naive baselines can't solve it), and (c) not impossible (the ground truth is computable and the problem is solvable in principle).

### 6.2 Stage 1: Hard Gates (automatic, zero cost)

Applied to every problem. Instant accept/reject.

| Gate | Check | Reject if |
|------|-------|-----------|
| Parameter bounds | All params within declared family's valid range | Out of range |
| Trajectory stability | System doesn't diverge or collapse to NaN | Unstable |
| Attractor bounds | Values stay within expected range | Escape |
| Minimum λ_max | λ_max > 0.01 (for problems declared chaotic) | Not actually chaotic |
| Periodicity screen | Orbit not periodic with period ≤ 1000 (for chaotic problems) | Periodic window |
| Permutation entropy | PE of time series > threshold | No temporal structure (random-like) |
| Lag-1 autocorrelation | |ACF(1)| > threshold | No temporal dependence |

Problems targeting non-chaotic regimes (periodic, quasiperiodic, convergent) skip the λ_max and periodicity gates but still must pass stability and structure gates.

### 6.3 Stage 2: Baseline Battery (computation only, zero API cost)

Run naive strategies on each problem. The pass/fail logic per question type:

| Question type | Must PASS (above random) | Must FAIL (below ceiling) |
|---------------|--------------------------|---------------------------|
| CLASSIFY | At least one baseline > 30% | No baseline > 80% |
| IDENTIFY | At least one baseline > 1.5/N_families | No baseline > 70% |
| ESTIMATE | At least one baseline > 0.1 | No baseline > 0.6 |
| PREDICT | At least one baseline k_eff > 0 | No baseline k_eff > 3·τ_λ |
| RECONSTRUCT | N/A (baselines always get dim=1) | N/A |
| RELATE | At least one baseline > 25% | No baseline > 70% |

"Above random" confirms the data has exploitable structure. "Below ceiling" confirms naive strategies can't solve it. The valid zone is between these thresholds.

**Baseline strategies:**

| Baseline | Strategy | Applies to |
|----------|----------|------------|
| Persistence | x̂ = x_{T-1} | PREDICT |
| Mean reversion | x̂ = mean(x) | PREDICT |
| Linear AR(5) | Fit autoregressive, extrapolate | PREDICT |
| Histogram sampling | Sample from empirical distribution | PREDICT |
| Periodic extension | Repeat detected dominant period | PREDICT, CLASSIFY |
| Statistical moments | Compare mean/var/ACF | CLASSIFY, IDENTIFY, RELATE |
| Return map nearest-neighbor | Compare return map shape features | IDENTIFY, RELATE |
| False nearest neighbors | Estimate dimension from delay embedding | RECONSTRUCT |
| Parameter grid search | Simulate each candidate, match by ACF | ESTIMATE |

### 6.4 Stage 3: LLM Review (v1.5 only)

For the static benchmark (v1), problems are validated by Stages 1–2 only. Human spot-checking replaces LLM review.

For the self-evolving tournament (v1.5), Stage 3 triggers on borderline cases (~10% of proposals) where automated tests are ambiguous. A reviewer agent evaluates well-formedness, difficulty plausibility, and potential exploits. Binary accept/reject.

### 6.5 Oracle and Floor

Every problem bank includes two reference scores:

**Oracle solver**: Knows the true family and parameters. Simulates forward from estimated state. Provides the upper bound on performance. The gap between oracle and best agent = the "reasoning gap."

**Random floor**: Uniformly random answers (random class, random parameter, random predictions from data range). Provides the lower bound. The gap between best agent and random = the total signal.

**Best baseline**: The best-performing baseline from Stage 2. The gap between best agent and best baseline = the "reasoning signal above naive heuristics."

