## 9. Observation Model

### 9.1 Parameters

| Parameter | v1 Default | Range | Effect |
|-----------|-----------|-------|--------|
| T (n_points) | 200 | 50–500 | More = easier identification/estimation |
| σ (noise_std) | 0.01 | 0–0.1 | Higher = harder (masks structure) |
| stride | 1 | 1–10 | Higher = sparser, harder |
| dims_visible | "all" | "all" or subset | Partial observability (v1.5) |

### 9.2 Observation Noise is Non-Negotiable

Without noise, parameter estimation from clean 1D map data is trivially precise. For the logistic map: r = x_{n+1} / (x_n(1-x_n)). With 200 clean observations, δr < 10^{-10}. This collapses the difficulty gradient.

Calibrate σ so that parameter estimation precision is δp ~ 10^{-2} to 10^{-3} (meaningful but not trivial). For v1: σ = 0.01 as default, with problems at σ ∈ {0.005, 0.01, 0.02, 0.05} spanning the difficulty range.

### 9.3 Prediction Horizon

For PREDICT questions: K = ceil(5 × τ_λ) for chaotic systems. This ensures the horizon extends well beyond the Lyapunov time, so accurate prediction requires genuine model quality. For non-chaotic systems: K = 20 (fixed).

### 9.4 Noise Seed Reproducibility

Each problem instance is defined by: (system_spec, initial_condition_seed, noise_seed). Given these three values, the exact observation sequence is reproducible. All seeds stored in the database.

