## 15. Success Criteria

### For the Benchmark

- [ ] Produces reproducible Φ(n) curves across replicates (std of b < 0.1)
- [ ] Baseline battery correctly rejects naive strategies on all problem types
- [ ] Oracle score > best agent score > best baseline score > random floor (proper separation)
- [ ] Grammar depth 1 problems are harder than depth 0 (lower mean raw_score)
- [ ] At least one frontier model shows b > 1.05 on blocked condition

### For the NeurIPS Paper

- [ ] Clear evidence: blocked > shuffled (structural transfer exists, p < 0.05)
- [ ] Characterisation of which question types show strongest transfer effects
- [ ] Comparison across 2+ frontier model families with differential transfer capabilities
- [ ] Publishable Φ(n) curves with confidence intervals
- [ ] Anti-gaming analysis: formal argument + empirical demonstration
- [ ] Novel difficulty grounding (composite difficulty with h_KS) — no other benchmark has this

### For the Dissertation

- All NeurIPS criteria, plus:
- [ ] Reasoning trace analysis: what strategies do successful agents use?
- [ ] Failure mode taxonomy: how do agents fail on each question type?
- [ ] Cross-question transfer: does success on CLASSIFY improve IDENTIFY performance?
- [ ] Depth effect: quantify the performance drop from depth 0 → depth 1
- [ ] Calibration analysis: which models produce meaningful confidence estimates?

